  <new-page*><section|Introduction><label|sec:intro>

  For <math|<with|color|black|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>2<rsup|-\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>>>
  and <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|+>> let

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-R\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|d>\<assign\><around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|d>,<space|2em><with|color|black|\<bbb-T\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><rsup|d>\<assign\><around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|d>/<around*|(|M\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|d>>,<space|2em>\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>\<assign\><around*|{|\<varphi\>:\<bbb-T\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><rsup|d>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>|}>.
  </equation*>

  <math|> Define a probability measure <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>
  on <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,N>> by

  <\equation>
    \<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\><frac|exp<around*|(|-S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>|)>|Z<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>><big|prod><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><rsup|d>>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)><label|eq:measure>,
  </equation>

  where

  <\equation>
    S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>2\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><rsup|d>><around*|[|<frac|1|2>\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)>*<around*|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|s>\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)>+<frac|m<rsup|2>|2>*\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)><rsup|2>+<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)><rsup|4>-<frac|r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>|2>*\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)><rsup|2>|]><label|eq:action>.
  </equation>

  The normalisation constant <math|Z<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>\<gtr\>0> is
  chosen so that the total mass of <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>
  equals one. The parameters <math|m\<gtr\>0,\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0,r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
  are referred to as <with|font-shape|italic|mass>,
  <with|font-shape|italic|coupling constant> and <with|font-shape|italic|mass
  renormalisation>, respectively. The operator
  <math|<around*|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|s>> is the
  discrete fractional Laplacian of order <math|s\<in\><around*|(|0,1|]>>
  defined via functional calculus as the <math|s>-th power of the discrete
  nearest-neighbour Laplacian <math|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>. In
  order to pass to the limit, we define a probability measure
  <math|<wide|\<nu\>|^><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>> on
  <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>|)>> by embedding
  sample paths of <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>> in
  <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>|)>> via a suitable
  Fourier multiplier, see<nbsp><eqref|eq:embedding> below.

  For the sake of clarity, we restrict our considerations to <math|d=3>. Then
  <math|s=1> corresponds to the standard <math|\<Phi\><rsub|3><rsup|4>>
  model, while for fractional exponents <math|s\<gtr\>s<rsub|c>\<assign\>3/4>
  the model is subcritical, meaning that the nonlinear part can be treated as
  a perturbation of the Gaussian measure at small scales. Since reflection
  positivity is expected to hold only for <math|s\<leqslant\>1>, so we
  restrict our analysis to <math|s\<in\><around*|(|s<rsub|c>,1|]>>. For
  further discussion of the measure in<nbsp><eqref|thm:main>, we refer
  to<nbsp><cite|gubinelliGlobalSolutionsElliptic2019>. In what follows, we
  present a detailed analysis of the fractional regime
  <math|s\<in\><around*|(|3/4,1|)>>. The case <math|s=1>, corresponding to
  the classical Laplacian, can be handled by the same strategy with
  substantial simplifications. The main result of this paper is a proof of
  the following:

  <\theorem>
    \ <label|thm:main>Let <math|d=3> and fix
    <math|s\<in\><around*|(|3/4,1|]>>, <math|m\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>. <with|color|black|There exists a choice of
    mass renormalisation <math|<around*|(|r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>|)><rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>2<rsup|-\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>,M\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|+>>>
    such that <math|<around*|(|<wide|\<nu\>|^><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>|)><rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>2<rsup|-\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>,M\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|+>>>>
    is a tight family of probability measures on
    <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>|)>>. Any
    accumulation point <math|\<nu\>> of this family is non-Gaussian,
    invariant under translations, reflection positive, and satisfies

    <\equation>
      <big|int>e<rsup|\<theta\>*<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|(|1+<around*|\||\<bullet\>|\|>|)><rsup|-b>*
      <around*|(|1-\<Delta\><rsup|>|)><rsup|-a>\<phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>><rsup|4>>
      \<nu\><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<phi\>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>,<label|eq:int-bound>
    </equation>

    for sufficiently large <math|a,b\<gtr\>0> and sufficiently small
    <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    <with|color|black|Tightness is established in
    Sect.<nbsp><reference|sec:tightness> and the
    bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:int-bound> is proved in
    Sec.<nbsp><reference|sec:integrability>. The reflection positivity and
    translation invariance of any accumulation point follow as
    in<nbsp><cite|gubinelliGlobalSolutionsElliptic2019>, since these
    properties are inherited from the corresponding properties of the
    approximate measures <math|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>|)><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>.
    Non-Gaussianity follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:int-bound>, as Gaussian
    measures cannot integrate functions exhibiting super-exponential growth.>
  </proof>

  <\with|color|black>
    We expect that any accumulation point is invariant under all Euclidean
    transformations. Unfortunately, a direct characterisation of the limiting
    measures remains unavailable. As a result, we must deduce the properties
    of the accumulation points indirectly, relying on certain features of the
    approximating measures. Notably, since the measures on lattices are not
    rotationally invariant, establishing rotational invariance of the
    continuum limit poses a particularly challenging problem. It is
    conceivable that this issue could be addressed using the technique
    developed in<nbsp><cite|duchStochasticQuantizationTwoDimensional2024> in
    the context of <math|P<rsub|2><around*|(|\<Phi\>|)>> model. However,
    pursuing this direction lies beyond the scope of the present paper.

    Let us also mention the recent work<nbsp><cite|duchErgodicityInfiniteVolume2025>,
    where uniqueness of the limiting measure is established in small coupling
    regime for the standard <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> model. A similar
    strategy might extend to the fractional variant, providing a potential
    route toward a full verification of the OS axioms, at least in the regime
    of small coupling.
  </with>

  Our proof strategy introduces a novel combination of renormalisation group
  ideas and PDE techniques which we believe can be useful more widely in the
  context of the theory of subcritical singular SPDEs. The proof also applies
  to the vector version of the model where the field takes values in
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> for <math|n\<gtr\>1> and the functional
  <math|S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>> depends on
  <math|\<varphi\>> in an <math|O<around*|(|n|)>> symmetric way.
  <with|color|black|We briefly discuss how to adapt the proof to the vector
  case in Sec.<nbsp><reference|sec:vector>.>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:main> gives a construction of a model of
  Euclidean quantum field theory (EQFT) commonly referred in the literature
  as the <with|font-shape|italic|fractional <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>>
  model. This terminology stems from the form of the density appearing in the
  formula for the measure<nbsp><eqref|eq:measure> and the fact that the
  underlying space is three-dimensional. In the case <math|s=1>, the model
  reduces to the classical <math|\<Phi\><rsub|3><rsup|4>> theory, which has
  long been regarded as a crucial benchmark in constructive quantum field
  theory. Foundational results by Glimm and
  Jaffe<nbsp><cite|glimmPositivityPhiHamiltonian1973>, Feldman and
  Osterwalder<nbsp><cite|feldmanLambdaVarphiField1974|feldmanWightmanAxiomsMass1976>
  and other pioneers of EQFT laid the groundwork for demonstrating the
  existence of models satisfying the Wightman axioms for local relativistic
  QFT through probabilistic methods based on the Euclidean framework [GJ87].
  In the fractional regime, that is, for <math|s\<in\><around*|(|s<rsub|c>,1|)>>,
  the model we consider was introduced by Brydges, Mitter, and
  Scoppola<nbsp><cite|brydgesCriticalF42003> as a rigorous foundation for
  proving the existence of a non-trivial infrared fixed point via an
  <math|\<epsilon\>>-expansion for sufficiently small
  <math|\<epsilon\>=s-s<rsub|c>\<gtr\>0>. See
  also<nbsp><cite|brydges_non_gaussian_1998> for an analogous result
  concerning a related four-dimensional model.

  <with|color|black|Our proof also extends to the cases <math|d=1> or
  <math|d=2> and <math|s\<in\><around*|(|d/4,1|]>> without requiring any
  substantial modifications. We stress that we crucially need the condition
  of subcriticality (<with|font-shape|italic|i.e.> super-renormalizability),
  which translates into <math|s\<gtr\>d/4>, where <math|s> is the power of
  the fractional Laplacian. In particular, if <math|d\<geqslant\>4> this
  would require <math|s\<gtr\>1>. However, it is known that fractional
  Laplacian <math|<around*|(|-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|s>> with <math|s\<gtr\>1>
  does not satisfy the maximum principle, and as a result, our a priori
  estimate does not apply in this regime. Moreover, for <math|s\<gtr\>1> the
  measure is not expected to be reflection positive\Vsince the Gaussian part
  itself lacks this property\Vand is therefore of limited interest from the
  perspective of constructive quantum field theory. Finally, we note that for
  <math|d\<leqslant\>4> it was shown in<nbsp><cite|panisTrivialityScalingLimits2025>
  that at the criticality <math|s=d/4> as well as in the supercritical regime
  <math|s\<less\>d/4>, any possible continuum limit is trivial, in the sense
  that it is Gaussian.>

  In recent years there has been a renewed interest in EQFTs due to the
  development of an alternative approach to the proof of theorems like
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:main>. This new approach is grounded in the
  basic ideas of stochastic calculus and it is usually called
  <with|font-shape|italic|stochastic quantisation>. This term was introduced
  by Parisi and Wu<nbsp><cite|parisi_perturbation_1981> to describe the
  quantisation of gauge theories via the construction of a stochastic process
  evolving in fictitious time and whose stationary distribution is the target
  Euclidean QFT. This stochastic evolution is a nonlinear stochastic partial
  differential equation of a singular kind, for which a particular procedure
  of renormalisation is needed to give it a precise meaning. The analysis of
  such equations requires a mix of probabilistic and analytic arguments that
  escape the usual approach of It<wide|o|\<bar\>>'s stochastic differential
  equations. For this reason it took some time before the SPDE community
  learned how to handle such singular equation and discovered theories like
  regularity structures<nbsp><cite|hairer2014theory> and paracontrolled
  calculus<nbsp><cite|gubinelli2015paracontrolled|catellierParacontrolledDistributions3dimensional2018>
  or renormalisation group<nbsp><cite|kupiainen2016renormalization> which
  finally allowed to tackle the problem of the stochastic quantisation of the
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> model. Gubinelli and
  Hofmanová<nbsp><cite|gubinelliGlobalSolutionsElliptic2019> obtained the
  equivalent of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:main> with <math|s=1> and a small
  range of values below that using a mix of paracontrolled calculus for the
  small scale singularities of the equation and coercive estimates to tame
  the large scale fluctuations. We refer the reader to the introduction
  to<nbsp><cite|gubinelliGlobalSolutionsElliptic2019> for a deeper review of
  the literature and the history of constructive QFT and also to
  contextualise the meaning and consequences of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:main>.

  The probability measure <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:measure> is the equilibrium distribution of the Langevin
  dynamics governed by the finite system of SDEs

  <\equation>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>+\<lambda\>*<around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)><rsup|3><rsup|>-r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>*\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>=*\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>,<label|eq:dynamcs-eps-M>
  </equation>

  on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>\<assign\>\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<bbb-T\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M><rsup|d>>,
  where

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|t>+<around*|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|s>+m<rsup|2>
  </equation*>

  and <math|\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> is a spacetime white
  noise satisfying

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|t,x|)>*\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|s,y|)>|]>=\<delta\><around*|(|t-s|)>*\<bbb-1\><rsub|x=y>,<space|2em><around*|(|t,x|)>,<around*|(|s,y|)>\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>.
  </equation*>

  The constants <math|m\<gtr\>0,\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0,r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
  coincide with the parameters appearing in<nbsp><eqref|eq:action>. By
  standard stochastic analysis arguments, there exists a unique stationary
  solution <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:dynamcs-eps-M> and we have
  <math|Law<around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|t|)>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>
  for all <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. In what follows, we identify
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> and
  <math|\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> with periodic functions
  on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>:=<math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|d>>.

  The nontrivial step is now to control the solutions to the stochastic
  quantisation equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:dynamcs-eps-M> uniformly as
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<rightarrow\>0> and <math|M\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>.
  At small scales, it is expected that the nonlinear term in the dynamics is
  a perturbation of the linearised equation driven by spacetime white noise.
  Consequently, in the continuum limit, the solutions
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> are expected to
  converge to random distributions belonging to Besov spaces of negative
  regularity slightly worse than <math|s-d/2>, which is the regularity of the
  Gaussian free field. This low regularity presents a major analytical
  challenge, as it complicates the control of the nonlinear term.

  Inspired by the works of Wilson<nbsp><cite|wilson1971renormalization|wilson1974renormalization>
  and Polchinski<nbsp><cite|polchinskiRenormalizationEffectiveLagrangians1984>
  on the continuous renormalisation group and by the more recent approach
  introduced by one of the authors in<nbsp><cite|duchFlowEquationApproach2021|duchRenormalizationSingularElliptic2022>,
  we use a flow equation to effectively describe the solution
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> of the SPDE at some
  spatial scale much larger than <math|\<varepsilon\>> (see
  Kupiainen<nbsp><cite|kupiainen2016renormalization> for a discrete
  counterpart). Let <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  denote a description of the solution at a scale of order

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>><rsup|>\<assign\><around*|(|1-\<sigma\>|)>\<gg\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0
  </equation*>

  for some <math|\<sigma\>\<in\><around*|(|0,1|)>>. The flow equation
  approach consists in deriving a parabolic equation for
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>> of the
  form

  <\equation>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>=F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>.<label|eq:effective-spde>
  </equation>

  Here, <math|\<psi\>\<mapsto\>F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>>
  is an analytic functional depending on the noise
  <math|\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>, called the
  <with|font-shape|italic|effective force>, such that

  <\equation>
    F<rsub|1><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>=-\<lambda\>*\<psi\><rsup|3>+r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>*\<psi\>+\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>.<label|eq:final-cond>
  </equation>

  In particular, <eqref|eq:dynamcs-eps-M> can be recovered
  from<nbsp><eqref|eq:effective-spde> for <math|\<sigma\>=1> and
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|1>=\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>.
  The functional <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  can be obtained by solving a <with|font-shape|italic|flow equation>

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>=\<bbb-B\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>,F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)>,<label|eq:flow-intro>
  </equation>

  <with|font-shape|italic|backwards> for <math|\<sigma\>\<in\><around*|(|\<mu\>,1|]>>
  with the final condition<nbsp><eqref|eq:final-cond> and where
  <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|\<sigma\>>> is an appropriate bilinear operator. The
  parameter <math|\<sigma\>\<in\><around*|[|0,1|]>> does not have any
  specific physical meaning and the spatial scale of the decomposition is
  fixed conventionally to be of order <math|<around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>>>,
  that is <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  is expected to fluctuate at spatial scales of order
  <math|<around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>>> or larger, and in
  particular to be a locally bounded function on
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|0>\<assign\>\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
  when extended in some reasonable way from the lattice
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> to the continuum. A key ingredient
  is the control of the stochastic process
  <math|<around*|(|F<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  solving the flow equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:flow-intro>. Following a simple
  but crucial observation of<nbsp><cite|duchFlowEquationApproach2021|duchRenormalizationSingularElliptic2022>,
  this control can be obtained by studying the evolution in the scale
  parameter of the cumulants <math|<around*|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  of the process <math|<around*|(|F<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>,
  which themselves satisfy a kind of higher-order deterministic flow equation

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>=\<cal-A\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)>+\<cal-B\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>,\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)>,<label|eq:flow-cumul-intro>
  </equation>

  with prescribed initial condition <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|1><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>.
  Upon choosing appropriately this initial condition by tuning the parameter
  <math|r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>> in <eqref|eq:final-cond> one can prove
  uniform in <math|\<varepsilon\>> and <math|M> estimates for the cumulants
  <math|<around*|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  and therefore, by a Kolmogorov-type argument, suitable bounds on the
  effective force <math|<around*|(|F<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  uniform as <math|\<varepsilon\>\<rightarrow\>0> and
  <math|M\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>.

  The flow equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:flow-intro> is bilinear and therefore
  solvable in general only in a perturbative regime, e.g. in a small interval
  <math|I=<around*|[|<wide|\<sigma\>|\<bar\>>,1|]>> around the initial
  condition at <math|\<sigma\>=1> or for small data (or small time). The size
  of this perturbative region depends crucially on the size of the noise
  <math|\<xi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> and while this
  dependence can be made uniform in <math|\<varepsilon\>,M> there could be
  large fluctuations in the noise which make the region arbitrarily small and
  reduce the available proof of existence of solutions to
  <with|font-shape|italic|local> results. A similar limitation is present in
  the work of Kupiainen<nbsp><cite|kupiainen2016renormalization> who,
  instead, uses a discrete renormalisation group (RG) iteration, and more
  generally in all the other approaches which use an expansion of solutions
  in order to resolve the singular terms and control the limit as
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<rightarrow\>0>, e.g. in regularity structures and
  also in paracontrolled calculus. This difficulty is the signal of the
  \Plarge field problem\Q, well known in constructive EQFT.\ 

  From the point of view of the stochastic quantisation equation, the
  large-field problem can be addressed by exploiting the coercivity of the
  nonlinear term which pulls the solution back from infinity. While this
  observation is standard in PDE theory, it still requires some nontrivial
  adaptation to be effective for singular SPDEs. The first to solve the
  problem have been Mourrat and Weber<nbsp><cite|mourrat2017global> in their
  proof of global existence for the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> dynamics
  on the full space with the usual Laplacian diffusion term and subsequently
  Gubinelli and Hofmanová in the context of paracontrolled analysis of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> models<nbsp><cite|gubinelliGlobalSolutionsElliptic2019|GH21>
  including the parabolic three dimensional setting. Moinat and
  Weber<nbsp><cite|MoinatWeber2020> proved the so called spacetime
  localisation property for the dynamic <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> model
  in the framework of regularity structures. This result was further extended
  by Chandra, Moinat, and Weber<nbsp><cite|chandraPrioriBoundsPhi2023> to
  cover the full subcritical regime. In the latter work, the authors modify
  the covariance of the noise, rather than the diffusion term, in order to
  explore regularities arbitrarily close to the critical threshold. Although
  their estimates suffice to establish tightness of the invariant measures
  associated with their SPDE, these measures are not explicit, and is not
  cleat if they are reflection positive. Consequently, their role in the
  stochastic quantisation of Euclidean QFTs is, at present, not fully
  understood.\ 

  In the broader context of global solutions for singular SPDEs, we also
  mention the recent preprint by Chandra, Feltes, and
  Weber<nbsp><cite|chandraPrioriBounds2d2024>, which establishes results for
  the stochastic quantisation of the two-dimensional sine-Gordon model on a
  periodic domain for parameter values slightly above the first threshold.
  This has since been extended up to the second threshold
  in<nbsp><cite|bringmannGlobalWellposednessDynamical2025>.
  Moreover,<nbsp><cite|bringmannGlobalWellposednessStochastic2024> proves
  long-time well-posedness of the two-dimensional Abelian Higgs model.

  <\with|color|black>
    During the revision of the present paper, a new
    preprint<nbsp><cite|esquivelPrioriBoundsDynamic2024> appeared in which a
    priori bounds for the fractional <math|\<Phi\><rsub|3><rsup|4>> model on
    the three-dimensional torus were established in the full subcritical
    regime. The main distinctions between our results and those
    of<nbsp><cite|esquivelPrioriBoundsDynamic2024> are that we employ the
    flow-equation framework and work on an infinite lattice,
    whereas<nbsp><cite|esquivelPrioriBoundsDynamic2024> use the regularity
    structures approach and study the continuum model on a torus with
    mollified noise. As far as we know, mollification of the noise is not a
    feasible strategy for establishing stochastic quantisation of an EQFT, in
    particular with respect to reflection positivity.
  </with>

  The main contribution of our work is the identification of a framework in
  which the flow equation method is combined with PDE estimates for the
  dynamics. This hybrid approach yields a powerful variant of the
  renormalisation group (RG). Instead of requiring an exact solution to the
  flow equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:flow-intro>, it suffices to construct a
  suitable approximate solution <math|<around*|(|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>=<around*|(|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:final-cond> for which the quantity

  <\equation>
    H<rsub|\<sigma\>>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>-\<bbb-B\><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>,F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>|)>,<label|eq:approx-flow>
  </equation>

  is small enough in an appropriate sense. The price to pay for this
  approximation is a <with|font-shape|italic|remainder> term
  <math|R<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>> in the SPDE
  which now reads as a <with|font-shape|italic|system> of two equations:

  <\equation>
    <choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>=J<rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|[|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>+R<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>R<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>=H<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>+\<mathD\>F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><around*|(|<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>G<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>R<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|>|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|1><rsup|>=0,>>>>><label|eq:effective-spde-remainder>
  </equation>

  for the pair of scale-dependent functions

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>,R<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>=<around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>,R<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|G<rsup|>\<assign\>\<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|-1>> is
  the fractional heat kernel, with <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|-1>>
  denoting the inverse of <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> as defined
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:ell-minus-one> below,
  \ <math|<around*|(|J<rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>> is a family of
  smoothing operators (see Def.<nbsp><reference|def:regularizing-kernels>)
  and

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|G<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\><around*|(|0,1|)>>\<assign\><around*|(|J<rsub|\<sigma\>>G|)><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\><around*|(|0,1|)>><rsup|>,
  </equation*>

  is a scale decomposition of <math|G>. Moreover,
  <math|\<mathD\>F<rsup|><around*|(|\<psi\>|)><wide|\<psi\>|~>> denotes the
  functional derivative of a functional <math|F> at <math|\<psi\>> in the
  direction of <math|<wide|\<psi\>|~>>.

  One can prove that the term <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>>
  retains the coercive structure of<nbsp><eqref|eq:final-cond>, that is,

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|\<sigma\>>F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>=-\<lambda\>
    \<phi\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|3><rsup|>+Q<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|Q<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>>
  is \Psmaller\Q than the cubic contribution provided
  <math|<around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>>\<ll\>1>. This
  together with the linearity in <math|R<rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  of the second equation of<nbsp><eqref|eq:effective-spde-remainder> make
  this system amenable to standard PDE techniques: by choosing
  <math|<around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>>\<ll\>1> one can
  control the non-coercive part <math|Q<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>><around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>>
  of the effective force using the coercive part <math|-\<lambda\>
  \<phi\><rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|3><rsup|>>, thereby resolving the large-field
  problem. At the same time, for <math|<around*|\<llbracket\>|\<sigma\>|\<rrbracket\>>\<gtr\>0>,
  we have uniform estimates for <math|Q<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>,\<mathD\>F<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>,H<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
  as <math|\<varepsilon\>\<rightarrow\>0> and
  <math|M\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>, provided the renormalisation constant
  <math|r<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>> appearing in the boundary
  condition<nbsp><eqref|eq:final-cond> for the effective force is chosen
  appropriately. This allows the full control
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:dynamcs-eps-M> and the proof of tightness of the laws of
  the processes <math|<around*|(|\<phi\><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>|)><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>,
  and therefore of the family of measures
  <math|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>|)><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,M>>.

  The implementation of this plan has to deal with two main technical
  difficulties:

  <\enumerate-alpha>
    <item>The scale-by-scale decomposition
    <math|<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>G<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
    of \ the fractional heat kernel <math|G<rsup|>>
    (cf.<nbsp><eqref|eq:est-kernel-G>) produces kernels with limited
    spacetime decay (see Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:Gdot-decay>), reflecting
    the restricted smoothness of <math|G> away from the origin. This
    algebraic decay of <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<sigma\>>G<rsup|><rsub|\<sigma\>>>
    propagates to the kernels of the effective force, necessitating a careful
    choice of weighted spaces for both the solutions and the kernels. To
    handle this limited decay, spacetime localisations of various operators
    and kernels are employed in several places\Vmost notably in the
    localisation procedure for the relevant cumulants, which governs the flow
    of the renormalisation constants, see
    Appendix<nbsp><reference|app:localization>. It would be interesting to
    devise an alternative strategy to bypass this problem with some other
    scale decomposition (or an additional localisation procedure).\ 

    <item>The natural setting of the analysis provides only negative
    spacetime regularity for the solutions of the SPDE. Such regularity is
    insufficient for stochastic quantisation, since one must be able to
    compute the marginal of the solution at a given time. To address this, it
    is necessary to work with distributional norms defined via smoothing
    operators <math|<around*|(|K<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsub|\<mu\>>> that provide
    only limited time regularisation. Suitable Schauder estimates for these
    operators can be established (see Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:schauder-full>).
    These smoothing operators also influence the definition of the norms for
    the kernels of the effective force (cf.
    Def.<nbsp><reference|def:norm-kernels>) and require a careful adaptation
    of the Kolmogorov-type argument used in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:v-and-K>.
  </enumerate-alpha>

  \;

  <no-indent><with|font-series|bold|Comparison with other approaches.> The
  possibility of working with an approximate flow equation makes it easier to
  compare the RG approach advocated in this paper (and originally proposed
  in<nbsp><cite|kupiainen2016renormalization>
  and<nbsp><cite|duchFlowEquationApproach2021|duchRenormalizationSingularElliptic2022>)
  with regularity structures<nbsp><cite|hairer2014theory> and paracontrolled
  distributions<nbsp><cite|gubinelli2015paracontrolled>. There is a clear
  parallel among the various approaches. The flow equation constructs a
  random object <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  \V the scale-dependent <em|effective force> \V which encapsulates the
  influence of the noise and takes the form of a finite polynomial built from
  the noise and the linear part of the equation. This object corresponds
  respectively to the <em|model> in the theory of regularity structures, the
  <em|enhanced noise> in paracontrolled calculus, or the <em|rough path> in
  rough path theory.

  While <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> is
  obtained through a probabilistic construction, the remainder term
  <math|R<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>> is defined
  analytically in terms of <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>.
  This deterministic component mirrors the analytic machinery of regularity
  structures, the paracontrolled operators in the paracontrolled calculus,
  and the sewing lemma in rough path analysis.

  When the parameter <math|s> is near its critical value of
  <math|s<rsub|c>=3/4> the number of terms which have to be accounted for in
  the approximation <math|F<rsub|\<sigma\>><rsup|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>,M|)>>>
  of the solution of the flow equation grows in an unbounded manner. A
  notable advantage of the flow equation approach, however, lies in its
  relative insensitivity to this increasing complexity: the analysis remains
  compact and largely independent of the distance to criticality. This
  analytical efficiency \V the ability to capture the nonlinear propagation
  of randomness with minimal combinatorial overhead \V was first observed by
  Polchinski<nbsp><cite|polchinskiRenormalizationEffectiveLagrangians1984> in
  his proof of perturbative renormalizability of the Euclidean
  <math|\<phi\><rsup|4><rsub|4>> QFT. For a modern account of this approach
  to perturbation theory of QFTs, the reader can consult the book of
  Salmhofer<nbsp><cite|salmhoferRenormalizationIntroduction2007> or
  Kopper<nbsp><cite|kopperRenormalizationTheoryBased2007>.

  As we already noted, the application of RG ideas to SPDEs is made efficient
  by the observation of one of the authors<nbsp><cite|duchFlowEquationApproach2021|duchRenormalizationSingularElliptic2022>
  that flow equations can also be used to estimate cumulants directly,
  thereby avoiding explicit and cumbersome inductive arguments on trees \V
  much as Polchinski's method circumvents the inductive structure of BPHZ
  renormalisation. There are further conceptual similarities with recent
  developments by Otto, Weber, and collaborators<nbsp><cite|ottoPrioriBoundsQuasilinear2021|linaresDiagramfreeApproachStochastic2021>,
  who use PDE-based arguments to derive probabilistic estimates for the modes
  in regularity structures. The flow equation framework, however, offers an
  additional advantage: renormalisation conditions naturally appear as
  boundary conditions for the corresponding flow equation.

  The combination of the flow equation approach with stochastic quantisation
  in the context of the construction of EQFT has been recently exploited by
  Meyer and one of the authors in<nbsp><cite|gubinelliFBSDEApproachSineGordon2024>
  to study the sine\UGordon model in the full space up to the second
  threshold and, by De<nbsp>Vecchi, Fresta and one of the authors,
  in<nbsp><cite|devecchiStochasticAnalysisSubcritical2022>, to develop a new
  approach to Euclidean Fermionic theories. In both papers the stochastic
  quantisation is obtained by using a forward-backwards stochastic
  differential equation together with an approximate analysis of Polchinski's
  flow equation. <with|color|black|Finally, we mention the work
  <cite|duch2024construction>, where the Polchinski equation was employed to
  construct the Gross\UNeveu model, a critical fermionic model of Euclidean
  quantum field theory.>

  <\render-remark|Conclusions>
    Despite the technical difficulties due to the analysis of the fractional
    heat equation, we would like to stress that the present paper is
    self-contained and presents complete arguments for all proofs. Compared
    with the few existing works on singular SPDEs in the full subcritical
    regime, we believe our work is the first to address, in a unified
    framework, several intricate aspects of the problem:

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item>we present the entire argument \V both analytic and probabilistic
      \V in full detail;

      <item>we work with an extremely nonlocal SPDE posed on a
      (semi-)discrete space;

      <item>we establish an a priori bound in the full subcritical regime,
      valid globally in spacetime;

      <item>we obtain optimal tail estimates for the solutions.
    </enumerate-alpha>

    We hope that this work illustrates the strength and flexibility of the
    stochastic quantisation approach in tackling, in a genuinely
    nonperturbative manner, the construction of Euclidean quantum field
    models in the subcritical regime.
  </render-remark>

  <\render-remark|Plan of the paper>
    In Sec.<nbsp><reference|sec:sq> we introduce the main objects of our
    analysis: the scale decomposition, the spacetime weighted norms which
    will be used to control the large fields and all the intermediate results
    which are needed in the proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:main>. The
    coercive estimates will be proven in Sec.<nbsp><reference|sec:apriori>,
    while the approximate flow equation for the effective force will be
    analysed in Sec.<nbsp><reference|sec:flow> via the flow equation for the
    cumulants. Appendix<nbsp><reference|app:technical> contains some
    technical lemmas and Schauder estimates tailored to our setting while
    Appendix<nbsp><reference|app:flow-eq> contains the detailed definition of
    the various contributions to the flow equations for the cumulants and
    their analytic estimates.
  </render-remark>

