      <|db-entry>
        <db-field|author|Romain <name|Panis>>

        <db-field|title|Triviality of the scaling limits of critical Ising
        and <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> models with effective dimension at least
        four>

        <db-field|year|2026>

        <db-field|number|2>

        <db-field|eprint|2309.05797>

        <db-field|primaryclass|math>

        <db-field|publisher|arXiv>

        <db-field|doi|10.1214/25-AOP1782>

        <db-field|urldate|2025-10-10>

        <db-field|abstract|We prove that any scaling limit of a critical
        reflection positive Ising or $\\\\\\\\varphi\B4$ model of effective
        dimension $d_{\\\\\\\\text{eff}}$ at least four is Gaussian. This
        extends the recent breakthrough work of Aizenman and Duminil-Copin \U
        which demonstrates the corresponding result in the setup of
        nearest-neighbour interactions in dimension four \U to the case of
        long-range reflection positive interactions satisfying
        $d_{\\\\\\\\text{eff}}=4$. The proof relies on the random current
        representation which provides a geometric interpretation of the
        deviation of the models' correlation functions from Wick's law. When
        $d=4$, long-range interactions are handled with the derivation of a
        criterion that relates the speed of decay of the interaction to two
        different mechanisms that entail Gaussianity: interactions with a
        sufficiently slow decay induce a faster decay at the level of the
        model's two-point function, while sufficiently fast decaying
        interactions force a simpler geometry on the currents which allows to
        extend nearest-neighbour arguments. When $1\\\\\\\\leq d\\\\\\\\leq
        3$ and $d_{\\\\\\\\text{eff}}=4$, the phenomenology is different as
        long-range effects play a prominent role.>

        <db-field|archiveprefix|arXiv>

        <db-field|keywords|Mathematical Physics,Mathematics - Mathematical
        Physics,Mathematics - Probability>

        <db-field|journal|The Annals of Probability>

        <db-field|volume|54>

        <db-field|pages|892\U972>
      </db-entry>

      <\db-entry|+1JU0xiOO5D2lzI|article|gubinelliFBSDEApproachSineGordon2024>
